(p_week | p_month) / 
(p_quarter | p_year) +
  plot_annotation(title = "Time Aggregation Effect on JSE Top 40 Volatility")









Weekly returns retain some of the short-term fluctuations visible in the daily series. As the aggregation period increases, fewer observations remain, and individual daily movements become less visible.
The smoother appearance of the lower-frequency plots does not necessarily imply lower volatility. These plots show cumulative returns over different horizons, and their scales differ. In addition, the first and last aggregated periods may be incomplete because the dataset begins and ends within a calendar period.

Importing GDP and Unemployment Data
GDP and unemployment observations are grouped by calendar year. The mean of the available observations within each year is used to construct each annual series.


Click to expand code
# importing and aggregating gdp data to annual because there is only annual data
gdp_dat &lt;- read_csv("real gdp.csv")
gdp_annual &lt;- gdp_dat |&gt;
  mutate(Year = year(observation_date)) |&gt;
  group_by(Year) |&gt;
  summarise(GDP = mean(NGDPRNSAXDCZAQ, na.rm = TRUE))

# importing unemployment data and aggregating to year 
unemp_dat&lt;- read_csv("unemployment rate.csv")
unemp_annual &lt;- unemp_dat|&gt;
  mutate(Year = year(observation_date)) |&gt;
  group_by(Year) |&gt;
  summarise(Unemployment = mean(LRUN64TTZAQ156S, na.rm = TRUE))


The GDP series definition and units should be confirmed from its source metadata before interpreting it as real GDP. In particular, real GDP, nominal GDP, and GDP growth are different measures and should not be used interchangeably.
